agent_message[8]=The first invariant attempt exposed a representation issue, not an algorithmic one: `intVals(IS) = .ValSeq` did not let the prover invert the embedding to `IS = .IntSeq`. I’m removing that avoidable indirection. The repaired theorem ranges directly over the semantics’ `ValSeq` and uses an explicit `allInts` precondition, so constructor facts arise from the real list being iterated. No operational rule or oracle is being added.
agent_message[9]=The repaired residual is now a genuine semantics branch: because the invariant framed an arbitrary local-map remainder, the prover had to consider `"$cells"` and closure-cell assignment, even though `move_one_ball` is a module-level capture-free function. I’m narrowing the invariant to the exact plain-frame domain with `"$cells" ∉ M`; this matches the call rule’s allocated frame and removes only an unreachable binding mode.
agent_message[10]=The map-membership solver still cannot refute the synthetic cell branch through an abstract map frame. The fixed call rule gives us a cleaner solution: at this loop head the local scope has exactly five names (`arr`, `first`, `previous`, `current`, `drops`) and parent `0`. I’m making that exact configuration part of the invariant. This also strengthens Gate A context containment and is why `current` was initialized before the loop.
agent_message[11]=The next residual is precise: the reference has the correct integer comparison equation, but the prover does not refine a `Val`-sorted iterator element from the path fact `isInt(V)`. I’m adding a guarded simplification lemma that is exactly the existing integer `applyCmp("<", I, P) = I <Int P` equation after sort projection. It is a derived lemma (not a k-cell rewrite), overlaps the base equation only with the same result, and is restricted to two values proven to be integers.
agent_message[12]=The comparison lemma compiled, but K rejects simplification rules whose head is the generated sort predicate `isInt`. I’m replacing that attempted type lemma with a typed `lastInt` summary: it returns an `Int` only under the invariant’s integer-domain guard, while a separate `lastAfter` preserves the loop target as a `Val`. This keeps every equation truthful and removes the unsupported predicate rewrite.
agent_message[13]=The invariant is now inductive, but its empty case cannot prove a `Val` equals its projected `Int` even under `isInt(P)`. I’m keeping the actual local value unchanged in the base case and moving the type fact to a narrower derived comparison lemma for `applyCmp("<", A, lastAfter(P, VS))` under `isInt(A) ∧ isInt(P) ∧ allInts(VS)`. That proves only the exact comparison the post-loop code performs and avoids changing stored values.
